{
  "image_name": "0e7056956fccf55d4542d4f0622177ad.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the organization of the intestinal epithelium from the whole gut down to a single crypt–villus unit and the progression of cell types along this axis. It begins with the intestine, then zooms into tissue and finally to the epithelial lining, emphasizing how cells are arranged and renewed.\n\nThe main message is that intestinal cells originate in the crypt base, where the stem cell zone contains CBC stem cells intermingled with Paneth cells. Just above this region is a transit-amplifying zone, where progenitor cells proliferate and begin to commit to different lineages. As these cells migrate upward along the crypt and onto the villus, they differentiate into mature epithelial cell types. The mature surface includes absorptive enterocytes and several secretory cell types, such as goblet, enteroendocrine, and tuft cells. Apoptotic cells are shown near the villus tip, indicating cell loss at the end of the upward migration.\n\nOverall, the diagram presents the intestine as a continuously renewing epithelium with spatially organized stem cells, progenitors, differentiation, migration, and shedding from crypt bottom to villus top.",
